{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L8dN46HZ4kXFu5VwuWp4sy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK, with default flags (header=n, body=t, footer=n), both produce the same output. That's good.\n\nNow, the issue with `-h a` is that gnl shows body1 as 1 but our nl shows body1 as 3. This means gnl resets the counter when the body section starts, even though R4.2 says only header triggers a reset.\n\nWait, actually let me think about this again. When no `-h a` is used, header lines are not numbered (style n), so the counter stays at the startNum (1). \n\nThen body1 gets 1 and body2 gets 2. That matches. But with `-h a`, the header lines ARE numbered. header1 gets 1, header2 gets 2. Counter is now 3. Then body starts - gnl resets to 1 but our implementation continues from 3.\n\nSo the question is: does gnl reset the counter at each section delimiter? I need to test this more carefully - maybe it's not just the body delimiter causing the reset, but every section transition. Let me check what happens with `-h a` and `-f a` together, or test `-h a` without a body delimiter to isolate whether it's the delimiter itself or something else triggering the reset.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3063,"cache_read_input_tokens":33383,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3063},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5644f380-9330-4225-b434-586ae7f3724b","uuid":"748661bb-3b6f-4201-bc7e-1758cf3627a9"}
